        <h2>Sri Lanka's Gem Capital</h2>
        <p>Ratnapura has been producing precious stones for over 2,000 years. When Marco Polo sailed past Ceylon in 1293, he recorded "rubies, sapphires, topazes, amethysts and many other precious stones" — a reputation the island has sustained ever since. The reason lies beneath the Sabaragamuwa basin, a prehistoric lagoon whose alluvial deposits concentrated gemstones over millions of years into one of the richest gem-bearing regions on earth. The numbers speak for themselves: the blue star sapphire <em>Star of India</em> (563 carats, now in the American Museum of Natural History in New York) came from Sri Lanka. The <em>Blue Belle of Asia</em> sapphire (392 carats) sold at Christie's for USD 17.3 million. The <em>Logan Sapphire</em> (423 carats, Smithsonian Institution) is Sri Lankan. Today, Sri Lanka exports over USD 500 million worth of gems annually, and Ratnapura — whose very name translates as City of Gems (Ratna = gem, Pura = city) — is where most of this originates.</p>

        <h2>What Gems Come from Sri Lanka</h2>
        <p><strong>Blue sapphires</strong> are the crown jewel of Sri Lankan gemology. The cornflower-blue "Ceylon sapphire" is among the world's most prized coloured stones — lighter and more transparent in tone than the darker Burmese equivalents, with an exceptional clarity. Beyond blue, Sri Lanka produces star sapphires (with a six-pointed asterism), yellow sapphires, pink sapphires and padparadscha sapphires — an extremely rare pinkish-orange variety found almost exclusively here. <strong>Rubies</strong>, though less famous than Burma's, are mined in the Ratnapura district. <strong>Cat's eye chrysoberyl</strong> — the chatoyant honey-coloured stone that appears to have a moving slit of light across its surface — has its world primary source in Sri Lanka. <strong>Alexandrite</strong> (colour-changing from green in daylight to red under incandescent light) and <strong>spinel</strong> are also found here: the "Black Prince's Ruby" in the British Imperial State Crown is, in fact, a Sri Lankan spinel. The Ceylon designation on a GIA or AGL certificate is widely recognised as a quality marker that commands a premium.</p>

        <h2>Gem Mining in Ratnapura</h2>
        <p>The gem mines of Ratnapura are artisanal, family-run operations scattered through the rice paddies and riverbeds surrounding the town. The technique — called <em>illam</em> mining — has been unchanged for centuries: workers dig vertical shafts into the alluvial gravel deposits (the gem-bearing layer, called illam, typically sits 3–9 metres below the surface), haul up the gravel in baskets, and sift it through sieves in water to separate the heavier gem material from the lighter clay. A good day's sifting might yield a handful of rough stones, most low quality, a few worth cutting and grading. You can visit working mines in the fields around Ratnapura — many families welcome visitors for a small tip, and watching the process is genuinely fascinating. Several tour operators in Colombo and Ratnapura offer structured half-day tours covering a mine visit, a cutting and polishing workshop (where rough stones are shaped on grinding wheels and polished to their final form), and a guided walk through the gem bazaar — a solid introduction to the full supply chain.</p>

        <h2>The Gem Bazaar</h2>
        <p>The informal gem market that clusters around Ratnapura's main street and the banks of the Kalu Ganga river is one of Sri Lanka's most atmospheric daily spectacles. Dealers arrive in the morning with small paper parcels of rough and cut stones, spreading them on white cloths on low tables or held in the palm of a hand, examining each other's merchandise through loupe magnifying glasses, negotiating in whispers. The trade is conducted entirely by trust and reputation — the same dealer networks have operated for generations, and a stone's provenance is tracked through informal memory as much as documentation. Even if you have no intention of buying, spending an hour at the bazaar watching this centuries-old market operate is one of the more quietly extraordinary travel experiences in Sri Lanka. Bring a magnifying glass if you have one — dealers will cheerfully let you examine stones.</p>

        <h2>How to Buy Gems Safely</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka's gem market has a well-earned reputation for both extraordinary value and elaborate scams. The rules for buying safely are simple but absolute:</p>
        <p><strong>Never buy from street touts, tuk-tuk drivers, or unsolicited "friends".</strong> The classic scam runs as follows: a friendly driver or stranger offers to take you to "his uncle's gem shop" or a "government gem school" — the shop sells glass or synthetic stones at genuine-gem prices. The presentation is convincing; the certificates are forged. Walk away.</p>
        <p><strong>Buy only from National Gem and Jewellery Authority (NGJA) licensed dealers.</strong> Licensed shops display their NGJA registration certificate. The <strong>Sri Lanka Gem & Jewellery Corporation</strong> in Colombo (a state-run enterprise) is the single most trustworthy retail option for visitors — prices are not the lowest, but authenticity is guaranteed. For the Ratnapura market, ask your guesthouse or hotel for a recommended licensed dealer introduction.</p>
        <p><strong>Always request a certificate.</strong> For any purchase above USD 200, request a GIA (Gemological Institute of America) or NGJA certificate of authenticity. Legitimate dealers carry them; the absence of one is a red flag. Budget ranges: USD 20–50 for small semi-precious stones and low-grade sapphires; USD 200–2,000 for quality mid-range sapphires; fine stones in exceptional colours run to tens of thousands. Cutting and setting in custom jewellery is available from Colombo workshops at competitive prices — allow 2–3 days.</p>

