        <h2>The National Gem Museum</h2>
        <p>The Ratnapura National Museum on Ehelapola Mawatha is an underrated stop before hitting the gem market. Its collection includes significant rough and cut specimens — a famous star cat's eye exhibit, geological cross-sections showing the alluvial gravel formations that contain gem deposits, and historical documentation of the gem trade stretching back to the Anuradhapura period. Entry is free or requires a nominal fee. The museum is open Tuesday to Sunday, typically 9am–5pm, and closed on public holidays. Spending 45 minutes here before the gem bazaar gives useful grounding — you'll understand what you're looking at, which makes you a harder target for misrepresentation.</p>

        <h2>Bopath Ella Waterfall</h2>
        <p>Three kilometres from Ratnapura town centre, Bopath Ella is one of Sri Lanka's most distinctive waterfalls — named for its shape, which resembles a bo leaf (the sacred fig leaf of the Bodhi tree). The waterfall drops around 30 metres into a rock pool that is safe for swimming during moderate water conditions. A short 15-minute walk from the road through a forested path leads to the base. The falls are most dramatic after rain, when the volume swells and the mist carries well beyond the pool. Entry is through a small park gate (nominal fee). Combining a morning at the Ratnapura gem bazaar with an afternoon at Bopath Ella makes a relaxed and varied half-day that shows two very different sides of Sabaragamuwa Province.</p>

        <h2>Sinharaja Rainforest</h2>
        <p>Thirty kilometres south of Ratnapura, <a href="/places/sinharaja">Sinharaja Forest Reserve</a> is Sri Lanka's last viable lowland tropical rainforest and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The forest is extraordinary: towering dipterocarps, rattan palms, tree ferns, and a canopy that supports 60% of Sri Lanka's endemic bird species — including the red-faced malkoha, Sri Lanka blue magpie and the green-billed coucal. A guided walk of 3–4 hours into the Kudawa entrance of the forest is the standard visit; guides are mandatory and can be hired at the forest entrance (approximately LKR 2,500–3,500 per group). The combination of Ratnapura gem market in the morning and Sinharaja in the afternoon is an excellent full day from Colombo — allow an early start (Colombo departure by 6am) and a knowledgeable driver who knows both locations.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Ratnapura</h2>
        <p>Ratnapura is 100km southeast of Colombo via the A4 highway — a journey of 2.5–3 hours by car depending on traffic through the suburban sprawl south of the city. Public buses run regularly from Colombo's Bastian Mawatha terminus (2.5–3 hours, LKR 200–300). There is no direct train service to Ratnapura; the nearest railway is at Avissawella (40km north), which connects to Colombo, but the bus is more convenient. Most visitors come on day trips from Colombo, often as part of guided gem tours (USD 60–100 per person including transport, mine visit, market and lunch). Ratnapura can also be combined with a south coast itinerary — it sits roughly between Colombo and the deep south, and the A4 continues through to Wellawaya and Ella, making it possible to pass through on a route from Colombo to the south without significant detour.</p>

